window.__hvBdRegister("plut-lives-4", {"key":"plut-lives-4","sections":[{"i":0,"j":14,"loc":"Life of Agesilaus, chs. 1–8","title":"Agesilaüs: upbringing, accession, and Lysander's eclipse","summary":"Agesilaüs, younger son of Archidamus, is raised in the Spartan agoge, learning to obey before he commands; he is small and lame but cheerful, and Lysander is his lover. When Agis dies, Lysander backs him for the throne against Leotychides, reputed son of Alcibiades, and turns the oracle about a 'maimed royalty' against the boy's bastardy. As king Agesilaüs courts the ephors and senators and so quietly increases his power, though he indulges his friends even in wrongdoing. Sent to Asia against Persia, he is offended at Aulis when the Boeotians stop his sacrifice, and at Ephesus he humbles the overbearing Lysander by making him his carver of meats.","ideas":["Spartan training makes citizens obedient; Agesilaüs alone among kings went through it","The lame-king oracle is reinterpreted as a warning against illegitimacy","Plutarch argues that rivalry spurs virtue but becomes harmful in excess","Agesilaüs' ambition clashes with Lysander's, to the harm of both"],"terms":[{"t":"agōgē","d":"The Spartan public course of training for boys, austere and aimed at obedience."},{"t":"ephors","d":"Annually elected Spartan magistrates whose office was meant to restrain the kings."}]},{"i":15,"j":29,"loc":"Life of Agesilaus, chs. 9–16","title":"Campaigns in Asia and the recall home","summary":"Agesilaüs answers Tisaphernes' perjury by feinting toward Caria and raiding Phrygia, then raises cavalry and defeats the satrap near Sardis; the King has Tisaphernes beheaded. Given command of the fleet too, he winters in Pharnabazus' province, gains Cotys of Paphlagonia as ally, and struggles with his love for the boy Megabates. A courteous meeting on the grass with Pharnabazus and his kindness to Pharnabazus' son show his manners, though he puts friendship above strict justice. Just as he plans to march into the heart of Persia, the ephors recall him to fight a Greek war, and he obeys at once; Plutarch praises the obedience and laments Greeks fighting Greeks. He forces his way home through Thrace, Macedonia and Thessaly.","ideas":["Deceiving a perjured enemy is presented as just and glorious","Simple living impresses Asia and humbles Persian satraps","Persian gold ('ten thousand archers') stirs Athens and Thebes against Sparta","Recall from Asia shown as model obedience to the state"],"terms":[{"t":"satrap (viceroy)","d":"A Persian provincial governor, such as Tisaphernes or Pharnabazus."}]},{"i":30,"j":40,"loc":"Life of Agesilaus, chs. 17–22","title":"Coroneia, homecoming, and the Corinthian War","summary":"Ordered to invade Boeotia, Agesilaüs hides news of Peisander's naval defeat at Cnidus and fights at Coroneia, where he attacks the Thebans head-on and is badly wounded, saved by his fifty volunteers. He dedicates a tithe at Delphi and returns to Sparta unchanged by foreign ways. At home he uses his sister Cynisca's Olympic chariot victory to show that such wins mark only wealth, suppresses Lysander's revolutionary speech, and wins over opponents and the young king Agesipolis. Campaigning near Corinth he disrupts the Isthmian games and snubs Theban envoys, but the destruction of a Spartan division by Iphicrates humbles him; he then fights in Acarnania.","ideas":["Agesilaüs' passion at Coroneia turns an easy win into a bloody struggle","He keeps the plain Spartan way of life after his return","He neutralizes rivals by favours rather than open attack","The loss to Iphicrates' targeteers checks his pride"],"terms":[{"t":"phiditium","d":"The Spartan common mess, where even the kings ate together."}]},{"i":41,"j":50,"loc":"Life of Agesilaus, chs. 23–28","title":"Peace of Antalcidas, Thebes, and the road to Leuctra","summary":"Sparta makes the shameful Peace of Antalcidas, handing the Asian Greeks to Persia; Agesilaüs uses it to weaken Thebes and defends Phoebidas' seizure of the Cadmeia on grounds of advantage, though he preaches that justice is the first virtue. When Sphodrias' failed raid on the Peiraeus brings him to trial, Agesilaüs, moved by his son Archidamus' love for Sphodrias' son Cleonymus, secures his acquittal and is blamed for it. Repeated invasions teach the Thebans to fight; a vein opened at Megara nearly kills him. At the peace congress Epaminondas demands equality, Agesilaüs strikes Thebes from the treaty, and twenty days later Sparta is defeated at Leuctra.","ideas":["Gap between Agesilaüs' professed justice and his conduct toward Thebes","Private affection sways a public trial","The allies-and-craftsmen demonstration of Spartan soldiery","Anger, not calculation, drives the Leuctra campaign"],"terms":[{"t":"Cadmeia","d":"The citadel of Thebes, seized by Phoebidas in peacetime."},{"t":"rhetra","d":"One of Lycurgus' ordinances; one forbade frequent wars against the same enemy."}]},{"i":51,"j":60,"loc":"Life of Agesilaus, chs. 29–34","title":"After Leuctra: Sparta invaded and defended","summary":"Sparta meets the news of Leuctra with composure, the kin of the dead rejoicing and those of survivors in shame. Many recall the oracle against a lame king, yet Agesilaüs is made lawgiver and lets the laws on cowards 'sleep for a day'. When Epaminondas invades Laconia with seventy thousand, Agesilaüs holds the city's heart without fighting, defuses a mutiny at the Issorium by a ruse, and executes conspirators without trial. Plutarch judges that his caution saved Sparta but could not restore her, since Lycurgus' polity was not built for empire. Archidamus wins the 'tearless battle', Messene is refounded, and Agesilaüs repels Epaminondas' surprise attack on Sparta, with the naked youth Isidas fighting conspicuously.","ideas":["Spartan dignity in defeat","Suspending the laws for one day to save both the laws and the men","Agesilaüs abandons contentiousness for a policy of safety","Loss of Messenia blamed on his reign"],"terms":[{"t":"tresantes","d":"Spartan term for those who fled in battle, subject to legal disgrace."}]},{"i":61,"j":71,"loc":"Life of Agesilaus, chs. 35–40","title":"Mantinea, Egyptian service, and death","summary":"Epaminondas falls at Mantinea, struck by the Spartan Anticrates. Agesilaüs refuses the general peace over Messenia and is thought headstrong and insatiable of war. Past eighty, he takes service as a mercenary captain under the Egyptian rebel Tachos, is mocked for his plain appearance, and resents being given only the mercenaries. He goes over to Nectanabis with Sparta's private approval, an act Plutarch calls treachery veiled by patriotism, then saves Nectanabis by a stratagem using the enemy's own siege trench. Sailing home with money, he dies at the Harbour of Menelaüs aged eighty-four, and his body is brought home in wax.","ideas":["Honourable action requires the fitting time and measure","Spartans judge justice by Sparta's glory","The trench stratagem compared to a wrestling trick","Forty-one years as king, leader of Greece until Leuctra"],"terms":[]},{"i":72,"j":91,"loc":"Life of Pompey, chs. 1–10","title":"Pompey's youth and rise under Sulla","summary":"Plutarch contrasts the hatred felt for Pompey's father Strabo with the people's lasting love for Pompey, citing his temperance, persuasive speech and tact, and his resemblance to Alexander. Anecdotes of Flora and of his plain eating follow. As a youth he foils a plot against his father and quells a mutiny; acquitted of embezzlement, he marries Antistia. At twenty-three he raises three legions in Picenum on his own authority, defeats Carbo's generals, and is hailed Imperator by Sulla. At Sulla's wish he divorces Antistia to marry Aemilia, who dies in childbirth, then recovers Sicily, where his treatment of Carbo is called needlessly insolent though he spares Himera.","ideas":["Pompey's popularity rests on character and bearing","A private citizen raises an army and wins Sulla's respect","The Sullan marriage shows tyranny's demands","Severity to Carbo balanced by clemency in Sicily"],"terms":[{"t":"Imperator","d":"Title of a victorious commander, hailed by troops; Sulla grants it to the young Pompey."},{"t":"Talasio","d":"Roman wedding cry, traced by Plutarch to the Sabine abduction."}]},{"i":92,"j":103,"loc":"Life of Pompey, chs. 11–16","title":"Africa, the name Magnus, and Lepidus","summary":"Pompey crosses to Africa, defeats Domitius in a storm, takes his camp, and subdues Numidia in forty days at twenty-four. When Sulla orders him to disband, his soldiers refuse to leave him; Sulla, learning the truth, greets him as 'Magnus'. Pompey demands a triumph though not even a senator, telling Sulla that more worship the rising sun, and wins it. He backs Lepidus for consul against Sulla's warning and is left out of Sulla's will, yet secures Sulla's burial. When Lepidus rises in arms, Pompey besieges Brutus at Mutina, has him killed after his surrender, and is blamed for it.","ideas":["Forty-day conquest of Africa","Origin of the surname Magnus","A triumph before becoming a senator","Lepidus' revolt crushed"],"terms":[{"t":"Magnus","d":"Surname 'the Great', given by Sulla or the army; Pompey used it only later."}]},{"i":104,"j":116,"loc":"Life of Pompey, chs. 17–23","title":"Sertorian War and the first consulship","summary":"Pompey keeps his army near Rome until sent to Spain to aid Metellus against Sertorius. He is humiliated at Lauron, defeats Herennius and Perpenna at Valentia, and fights an undecided battle at the Sucro, escaping by abandoning his horse; he defers to Metellus. After Sertorius is murdered, Pompey defeats Perpenna, executes him, and burns Sertorius' letters from leading Romans unread. Returning, he cuts off fugitives from Spartacus' army and claims to have ended that war, to Crassus' annoyance. Elected consul with Crassus, he restores the tribunate, appears before the censors declaring all his service done under himself, and is reconciled with Crassus at the end of the year.","ideas":["Sertorius' guerrilla war baffles regular commanders","Burning the letters to avoid new civil strife","Restoring the tribunate to win the people","Withdrawing from public view to guard his prestige"],"terms":[{"t":"tribunate","d":"Office of the people's tribunes, curtailed by Sulla and restored by Pompey."}]},{"i":117,"j":132,"loc":"Life of Pompey, chs. 24–29","title":"Command against the pirates","summary":"Plutarch describes the Cilician pirates: a thousand ships, four hundred captured cities, plundered sanctuaries, kidnapped praetors, and the mockery of Roman captives. The Gabinian law gives Pompey vast power over the sea and coasts; Catulus and others oppose it in vain, and prices fall at the news. Pompey divides the Mediterranean into thirteen districts, clears the western seas in forty days, and ends the war in under three months after victory at Coracesium. He spares the surrendered pirates and resettles them as farmers in Cilicia and at Dyme, but his interference against Metellus in Crete is widely blamed.","ideas":["Piracy as a disgrace to Roman supremacy","Unprecedented command under the Gabinian law","Human nature can be tamed by a change of life","Jealousy of Metellus mars his success"],"terms":[{"t":"Gabinian law","d":"Law proposed by Gabinius granting Pompey command against the pirates."}]},{"i":133,"j":146,"loc":"Life of Pompey, chs. 30–37","title":"Taking over the war against Mithridates","summary":"The Manilian law hands Pompey Lucullus' command against Mithridates and Tigranes; Pompey feigns weariness, but his friends see his pleasure. He undoes Lucullus' arrangements, and their meeting in Galatia ends in mutual abuse. Pompey routs Mithridates by moonlight near the Euphrates, the shadows deceiving the enemy, and the king flees with Hypsicrateia. Tigranes surrenders and is generously treated, while his rebellious son is chained. Pompey defeats the Albanians and Iberians in the Caucasus; Amazonian shields are found after a battle, and he returns Mithridates' concubines and accepts Stratonice's fortress; at Caenum he reads Mithridates' secret papers.","ideas":["Rivalry with Lucullus over glory","Moonlit night battle against Mithridates","Lenient settlement with Tigranes","Campaigns among the Caucasus peoples"],"terms":[{"t":"Manilian law","d":"Law of the tribune Manlius (Manilius) giving Pompey the Mithridatic command."}]},{"i":147,"j":158,"loc":"Life of Pompey, chs. 38–46","title":"Syria, Mithridates' death, and the third triumph","summary":"At Amisus Pompey settles provinces prematurely and marches to Syria to reach the Red Sea, blockading Mithridates by sea. He makes Syria a province, subdues Judaea, and indulges his freedman Demetrius. Near Petra he learns that Mithridates has killed himself, and later turns from the body. He honours Mitylene, Rhodes and Athens, divorces Mucia, and disbands his army on landing in Italy, to wide relief. After clashing with Cato over a marriage alliance and buying Afranius' election, he celebrates a vast triumph over Asia, his third over a third continent. Plutarch wishes he had died then.","ideas":["Ambition to touch the encircling Ocean","Mithridates' suicide ends the war","Disbanding the army disarms fears of tyranny","Triumphs over Libya, Europe and Asia"],"terms":[]},{"i":159,"j":173,"loc":"Life of Pompey, chs. 46–53","title":"Alliance with Caesar and the death of Julia","summary":"Plutarch argues that Pompey's power, used for others, raised up Caesar. Checked by Lucullus and Cato, Pompey relies on Clodius and abandons Cicero. Caesar reconciles Pompey and Crassus; Cato says their concord, not their later quarrel, ruined the state. Pompey backs Caesar's laws, marries Julia, and fills Rome with soldiers. Clodius humiliates him, and he recalls Cicero and receives the corn supply, crying that sailing is necessary, living is not. At Luca the three renew their pact; Pompey and Crassus take the consulship by violence and he opens his theatre. Julia's death and Crassus' fall in Parthia remove the bonds between Pompey and Caesar.","ideas":["Concord of the powerful as the first evil to the city","Pompey becomes the instrument of Caesar's policy","The Luca agreement divides provinces","Julia and Crassus had held the rivals in check"],"terms":[]},{"i":174,"j":188,"loc":"Life of Pompey, chs. 54–63","title":"Sole consul, the breach, and flight from Italy","summary":"Amid anarchy, Bibulus and Cato propose Pompey as sole consul to avoid a dictatorship. He marries Cornelia, restores order in the courts but shows partiality to his father-in-law Scipio, and keeps his provinces. He recalls the legions lent to Caesar; Italy's rejoicing at his recovery from illness breeds overconfidence, and he boasts that armies will spring up where he stamps. In the senate Curio's motion that both lay down arms carries, but the consuls appoint Pompey defender. Caesar crosses the Rubicon; Pompey is mocked for having no army, declares civil war, leaves Rome, and escapes from Brundisium by trenches and stakes, a move Cicero and Caesar criticize.","ideas":["Sole consulship as a legal alternative to tyranny","Overconfidence after Italy's festival for his health","The senate's vote for mutual disarmament overridden","Abandoning Italy for the East"],"terms":[{"t":"Interrex","d":"Temporary magistrate who held elections when there were no consuls."}]},{"i":189,"j":207,"loc":"Life of Pompey, chs. 64–72","title":"Dyrrachium and the defeat at Pharsalus","summary":"Pompey gathers a great fleet, cavalry and allies, training at Beroea despite his age; Cato's decree against plundering wins favour. Caesar lands, offers terms, and is beaten at Dyrrachium, but Pompey fails to press the victory. Pompey prefers to wear Caesar down by want, but taunts from his followers, already competing for offices, push him to battle in the plain of Pharsalia. He orders his line to receive the charge standing; Caesar's hidden cohorts strike at the faces of Pompey's cavalry, rout them, and turn his flank. Pompey walks back to his camp dazed, flees in disguise, and the festive camp is captured.","ideas":["A general yielding to the 'diseased passions' of his followers","Lament over Roman turning against Roman","Caesar's cohorts aiming at the cavalry's faces","Pompey compared to the stunned Ajax of Homer"],"terms":[]},{"i":208,"j":223,"loc":"Life of Pompey, chs. 73–80","title":"Flight to Egypt and murder","summary":"Pompey flees through Tempe to the sea and is taken aboard by the merchant Peticius, who had dreamed of him; Favonius serves him like a slave. At Mitylene he takes up Cornelia, who blames her own ill fortune, and argues with Cratippus about Providence. Regretting not using his fleet, he rejects Parthia on Theophanes' advice and sails to Egypt. Ptolemy's council under Potheinus, persuaded by Theodotus, decides to kill him because a dead man does not bite. Achillas and Septimius take him into a boat, where he is stabbed, a day after his birthday. His freedman Philip burns the body; Caesar weeps over the ring, punishes the killers, and Brutus later tortures Theodotus to death.","ideas":["The fall from mastery to a fisherman's hut","Cornelia's and Pompey's speeches on fortune","Egypt chosen over Parthia, with fatal result","A humble funeral for the greatest Roman imperator"],"terms":[]},{"i":224,"j":233,"loc":"Comparison of Agesilaus and Pompey, chs. 1–5","title":"Agesilaüs and Pompey weighed side by side","summary":"Plutarch sets the two men against each other point by point. Pompey rose to power justly and stayed loyal to Sulla, while Agesilaüs won his throne by disputing Leotychides' legitimacy, slighted Lysander, and shielded Sphodrias and Phoebidas out of partiality. Agesilaüs is praised for letting the laws 'slumber' after Leuctra and for leaving Asia at his country's call, and for holding Sparta against seventy thousand invaders; Pompey is faulted for fleeing Rome before Caesar and for being goaded into battle at Pharsalus. The comparison closes with their journeys to Egypt: Pompey went in flight and was betrayed, Agesilaüs went for money and betrayed those who trusted him.","ideas":["Pompey's rise was just; Agesilaüs's accession sinned against the oracle on the 'lame sovereignty'","Agesilaüs showed political virtue in suspending the laws after Leuctra and in recall from Asia","Pompey had more victories, but Agesilaüs was the better general in the decisive crises","A good general fights only when superior; Pompey let taunts force him to Pharsalus"],"terms":[{"t":"synkrisis (comparison)","d":"The formal side-by-side judgment that closes each pair of Plutarch's Parallel Lives."}]},{"i":234,"j":257,"loc":"Life of Alexander, chs. 1–10","title":"Birth, boyhood, Bucephalas and Aristotle","summary":"Plutarch opens by explaining that he writes Lives, not Histories, and seeks the signs of character in small acts. He recounts Alexander's descent from Heracles and Aeacus, the dreams and serpent surrounding Olympias, and the portents of his birth on the day the temple of Ephesian Artemis burned. The boy's appearance, temperament and ambition are described, along with his taming of Bucephalas and his education under Aristotle at Mieza, including a letter about the 'acroamatic' doctrines and his love of the Iliad. The section ends with his regency, Chaeroneia, quarrels with Philip over Attalus and Pixodarus, and Philip's murder by Pausanias.","ideas":["Small sayings reveal character better than battles","Portents of thunderbolt, lion-seal and serpent mark a more-than-human birth","Alexander tames Bucephalas by turning him from his shadow","Aristotle teaches him ethics, politics and secret doctrines; strife with Philip ends in Philip's murder"],"terms":[{"t":"acroamatic and epoptic","d":"Terms for the oral, esoteric teachings that philosophers shared with only a few pupils."},{"t":"Iliad of the Casket","d":"Aristotle's recension of the Iliad, which Alexander kept under his pillow with his dagger."}]},{"i":258,"j":269,"loc":"Life of Alexander, chs. 11–16","title":"Thebes razed, Diogenes, and the Granicus","summary":"Becoming king at twenty, Alexander rejects cautious advice and crushes the barbarians to the Danube, then marches on Thebes, which he takes and razes, sparing priests and Pindar's descendants. Timocleia, who killed the Thracian who violated her, is freed for her courage, and Athens is pardoned. At Corinth Diogenes asks him to stand out of his sun, and at Delphi the Pythia declares him invincible. He distributes his property, crosses the Hellespont, honours Achilles at Ilium, and wins the battle of the Granicus, saved from Spithridates' axe by Cleitus.","ideas":["Boldness, not concession, secures the new kingdom","The destruction of Thebes later causes Alexander remorse","'If I were not Alexander, I would be Diogenes'","At the Granicus Alexander charges first and nearly dies; Cleitus saves him"],"terms":[{"t":"Sacred Band","d":"The elite Theban troop whose ranks Alexander is said to have first broken at Chaeroneia."}]},{"i":270,"j":285,"loc":"Life of Alexander, chs. 17–23","title":"Gordium, the physician Philip, and Issus","summary":"Alexander subdues the coast of Asia Minor, passes along Pamphylia (a marvel he himself plays down), and at Gordium undoes the famous knot, by sword or by drawing the pin. Darius, encouraged by a dream, advances with six hundred thousand men while Alexander lies sick in Cilicia; Alexander drinks Philip the Acarnanian's medicine while handing him a letter accusing him of poison. At Issus the narrow ground favours Alexander, who routs the Persians and takes Darius' tent. Plutarch then dwells on his chivalry to Darius' captive mother, wife and daughters, his sexual restraint, his frugal appetite, and his habits over wine.","ideas":["The Gordian knot loosed, by cutting or by removing the pin","Trust in a friend: drinking the medicine while Philip reads the accusation","Victory at Issus: 'This, it would seem, is to be a king'","Self-mastery as more kingly than conquest of enemies"],"terms":[]},{"i":286,"j":301,"loc":"Life of Alexander, chs. 24–28","title":"Tyre, Alexandria and the oracle of Ammon","summary":"After Issus the Macedonians grow rich at Damascus, and Alexander besieges Tyre for seven months, guided by dreams of Heracles and a satyr; he also rescues his old tutor Lysimachus on a night raid. Tyre falls, Gaza is taken despite a shoulder wound, and Leonidas receives a lavish gift of incense. A dream of Homer's verses leads Alexander to found Alexandria at Pharos. He crosses the desert to Ammon, guided by rain and ravens, and is greeted as the god's son; Plutarch discusses his views on divine kingship and how he used belief in his divinity to rule barbarians rather than being deluded by it.","ideas":["Seven-month siege of Tyre, foretold by dreams","Homer as 'a very wise architect' for Alexandria","Ammon's prophet hails him as son of the god","God is father of all, but makes the best men his own"],"terms":[{"t":"Ammon","d":"The Libyan oracle-god identified with Zeus, whose prophet greeted Alexander as son."}]},{"i":302,"j":314,"loc":"Life of Alexander, chs. 29–35","title":"Darius' offers, Gaugamela, and the naphtha","summary":"Alexander holds dramatic contests in Phoenicia and rejects Darius' peace terms ('so would I, were I Parmenio'). Darius' wife dies, and the eunuch Teireos persuades Darius of Alexander's honourable conduct, so that Darius prays that only Alexander may succeed him. At Gaugamela Alexander refuses to 'steal' a night victory, sleeps soundly, and, armed and on Bucephalas, routs Darius' centre, though Parmenio's call for help lets Darius escape. Proclaimed king of Asia, he frees the Greek cities and honours Plataea and Croton; in Babylonia he marvels at naphtha, which nearly burns a boy alive, prompting a digression on its fiery nature.","ideas":["Parmenio's caution versus Alexander's ambition","Darius admires his enemy's self-restraint toward Persian women","'I will not steal my victory'","Naphtha as a possible explanation of Medeia's fire"],"terms":[{"t":"Gaugamela","d":"The site of the decisive battle, meaning 'camel's house', not Arbela as most writers say."}]},{"i":315,"j":327,"loc":"Life of Alexander, chs. 36–43","title":"Persian treasures, generosity, and Darius' death","summary":"Alexander seizes the treasures of Susa and Persis, ponders a fallen statue of Xerxes, and, urged by Thaïs at a revel, sets fire to the Persian palace, then repents. Plutarch gives examples of his generous, gracious giving, Olympias' warnings about his lavishness, and his rebuke of his friends' luxury, with the lion hunt as a model of toil. He shows his care for friends in his letters, but also how many accusations made him harsh in judging. Pursuing Darius, he refuses water his men cannot share; Darius is found dying, his last words thanking Alexander, who covers the body and later punishes Bessus.","ideas":["The burning of the palace at Thaïs's urging","Giving is a favour only when done with a kindly spirit","'It is a very royal thing to toil'","Refusing the helmet of water; Darius dies before Alexander arrives"],"terms":[]},{"i":328,"j":338,"loc":"Life of Alexander, chs. 44–49","title":"Persian dress, Roxana, and the fall of Philotas","summary":"In Hyrcania Alexander sees the Caspian and recovers the captured Bucephalas. In Parthia he adopts a mixed Persian–Median dress, and despite wounds crosses the Orexartes against the Scythians; Plutarch weighs the story of the Amazon queen and finds it doubtful. He tests and wins his army's loyalty, trains thirty thousand native boys in Macedonian ways, and marries Roxana. He uses Hephaestion with barbarians and Craterus with Greeks, reconciling their quarrel. Philotas, reported by his mistress Antigone as boastful, fails to pass on news of Limnus' plot, is tortured and executed, and Parmenio is killed, which makes Antipater afraid.","ideas":["Mixing customs to win goodwill rather than ruling by force","Hephaestion 'friend of Alexander', Craterus 'friend of the king'","The Amazon story rejected by many authorities","Philotas tortured and Parmenio killed"],"terms":[{"t":"proskynēsis (obeisance)","d":"The Persian prostration before the king, which Alexander's adoption of native dress may have been preparing the Macedonians to accept."}]},{"i":339,"j":358,"loc":"Life of Alexander, chs. 50–56","title":"The killing of Cleitus and Callisthenes' fall","summary":"At a drunken banquet, after mocking verses about defeated generals, Cleitus reproaches Alexander for disowning Philip and adopting Persian ways; Alexander, enraged, runs him through with a spear and then tries to kill himself. Callisthenes consoles him gently, but Anaxarchus tells him the king is himself the law, which eases his grief but makes him more lawless. Callisthenes' austerity and a speech denouncing the Macedonians earn him hatred, and his refusal of obeisance turns Alexander against him. After the plot of Hermolaüs he is accused, and dies by hanging or in chains; the aged Demaratus also dies.","ideas":["Anger and wine, not design, lead to Cleitus' death","Anaxarchus: Justice sits beside Zeus, so the ruler's acts are just","Callisthenes' refusal of obeisance spares the Greeks a disgrace","Alexander's later letter reveals hostility to Aristotle"],"terms":[{"t":"proskynēsis (obeisance)","d":"The prostration Alexander tried to introduce at court, which Callisthenes openly refused."}]},{"i":359,"j":371,"loc":"Life of Alexander, chs. 57–65","title":"India, Porus, and the naked philosophers","summary":"Before India Alexander burns the army's surplus baggage, and an oil-spring at the Oxus is read as a sign of glorious but toilsome campaigns. He takes steep citadels, competes in generosity with Taxiles, and massacres mercenaries under truce, which Plutarch calls a stain on his record. At the Hydaspes he crosses by night in a storm and defeats Porus, who asks to be treated 'like a king' and is given a larger realm; Bucephalas dies. The army refuses to cross the Ganges, and Alexander is nearly killed storming the Malli. He tests ten Gymnosophists with riddles and hears Calanus' lesson of the dry hide.","ideas":["Porus: 'Like a king'","The Macedonians refuse to cross the Ganges","Near death at the Malli citadel","Calanus' hide: hold down the centre of the empire"],"terms":[{"t":"Gymnosophists","d":"Indian 'naked philosophers' famed for short, sharp answers to hard questions."}]},{"i":372,"j":387,"loc":"Life of Alexander, chs. 66–77","title":"Return from India, Hephaestion, and Alexander's death","summary":"Alexander reaches the ocean, sends Nearchus by sea, and loses most of his army crossing the land of the Oreites, then revels through Carmania. He punishes faithless satraps, honours the tomb of Cyrus, watches Calanus burn himself on a pyre, and holds the mass weddings at Susa. His Macedonians resent the trained Persian youths, mutiny, and are reconciled in tears; then Hephaestion dies of fever at Ecbatana, and Alexander's grief is extreme. Omens and fear fill him with superstition in Babylon; after revelling with Medius he falls into a fever, which the 'Journals' trace day by day to his death. The section closes with the poison rumours, rejected by most writers, and Roxana's murder of Stateira.","ideas":["Disastrous march through the land of the Oreites","Mass marriages at Susa and the Macedonian mutiny","Grief for Hephaestion and the slaughter of the Cossaeans","Superstition as harmful as unbelief; death recorded in the 'Journals'"],"terms":[{"t":"Ephemerides (Journals)","d":"The royal court diaries that Plutarch follows almost word for word for Alexander's last illness."}]},{"i":388,"j":403,"loc":"Life of Caesar, chs. 1–10","title":"Young Caesar: Sulla, pirates, and early popularity","summary":"The Life opens with Caesar refusing Sulla's demand to divorce Cornelia, hiding from Sulla's men, and being captured by Cilician pirates, whom he later crucifies as he had promised. He studies oratory at Rhodes, wins fame as an advocate, and buys popularity with lavish spending, reviving the honours of Marius and winning the office of pontifex maximus against Catulus. In the debate on the Catilinarian conspirators he argues against execution without trial and is nearly killed leaving the senate. The section ends with the Bona Dea scandal: Clodius enters Caesar's house disguised as a woman, and Caesar divorces Pompeia because his wife ought not even to be suspected.","ideas":["Sulla sees in the boy many Mariuses","Caesar treats his pirate captors with mocking disdain, then crucifies them","Popular favour is bought by spending and the revival of Marius's images","Cicero suspects a tyrannical purpose beneath an agreeable manner"],"terms":[{"t":"pontifex maximus","d":"The chief priest of Rome, an elective office Caesar won against older and more distinguished rivals."},{"t":"Bona Dea","d":"The 'Good Goddess', whose nocturnal rites only women might attend, profaned by Clodius."}]},{"i":404,"j":411,"loc":"Life of Caesar, chs. 11–14","title":"Spain, the alliance with Pompey and Crassus, consulship","summary":"Crassus stands surety for Caesar's debts so that he can leave for Spain; on the way Caesar says he would rather be first in a poor village than second at Rome, and in Spain he weeps that he has not matched Alexander. He subdues the Callaici and Lusitani and settles debts between creditors and debtors. Giving up a triumph to stand for the consulship, he reconciles Pompey and Crassus, which Plutarch says, not their later quarrel, overthrew the aristocracy. As consul he passes land laws with Pompey's armed backing, marries Julia to Pompey, sidelines Bibulus and Cato, secures Gaul for five years, and helps Clodius drive Cicero out.","ideas":["Ambition shown in the village saying and the tears over Alexander","The friendship of Caesar and Pompey, not their enmity, began the civil wars","Marriage alliances used to trade powers and provinces","Cato's warnings judged morose then, wise later"],"terms":[{"t":"Imperator","d":"The acclamation given by soldiers to a victorious general, which Caesar received in Spain."},{"t":"in absentia","d":"Standing for office without being present in the city, which the senate, led by Cato, refused Caesar."}]},{"i":412,"j":427,"loc":"Life of Caesar, chs. 15–23","title":"Caesar in Gaul: soldiers, Rhine, and Britain","summary":"Plutarch judges that the Gallic wars show Caesar the equal of any Roman general, citing his vast numbers of cities, nations and enemies conquered. He illustrates the devotion of his soldiers, and Caesar's own hardiness despite a frail body and epilepsy, with anecdotes of his speed, dictation on horseback and plain manners. The campaigns follow: the Helvetii, Ariovistus and the Germans, the Belgae, and the near-disaster against the Nervii, saved by Caesar's personal charge and the tenth legion. Between campaigns he manages Roman politics from the Po; the meeting at Luca gives him five more years. He bridges the Rhine, crosses twice to Britain, and learns of Julia's death, which loosens the bond with Pompey.","ideas":["Caesar's men become invincible out of zeal for him","He makes campaigning a cure for his feeble health","He subdues enemies with citizens' arms and citizens with enemies' money","Julia's death dissolves the tie that held the state in concord"],"terms":[{"t":"Luca conference","d":"The meeting at which Pompey, Crassus and Caesar divided consulships and commands among themselves."}]},{"i":428,"j":437,"loc":"Life of Caesar, chs. 24–31","title":"Gallic revolts, Alesia, and the breach with Pompey","summary":"All Gaul rises: Titurius and Cotta are destroyed and Cicero's legion besieged until Caesar relieves it by feigning fear. The great revolt under Vergentorix follows; Caesar's winter march stuns the rebels, and at Alesia he builds a double wall and defeats a huge relief army, after which Vergentorix surrenders at his feet. With Crassus dead, Plutarch presents the rivalry of Caesar and Pompey as a contest for supremacy amid bribery and street violence at Rome, where Pompey is made sole consul. Caesar buys support in Rome and offers that both men lay down their arms together; the senate, swayed by Lentulus and Scipio, refuses, and Antony and Curio are driven from the city.","ideas":["Caesar's double wall at Alesia and the vanishing of the relief army","Vergentorix's ceremonial surrender","Caesar trained like an athlete far from his rival","The offer of joint disarmament and its rejection"],"terms":[{"t":"sole consul","d":"The office Cato had the senate give Pompey as a more legal monarchy than a dictatorship."}]},{"i":438,"j":452,"loc":"Life of Caesar, chs. 32–40","title":"Crossing the Rubicon and the war in Epirus","summary":"Sending a small force ahead to Ariminum, Caesar hesitates at the Rubicon, weighs the evils to come with his friends, and crosses saying 'Let the die be cast.' Panic sweeps Italy and Pompey abandons Rome with the consuls and senate. Caesar spares Domitius at Corfinium, masters Italy in sixty days, forces open the treasury against the tribune Metellus, subdues Pompey's legates in Spain, and as dictator passes reforms before crossing the Adriatic in winter. He tries to return to Brundisium in a small boat, telling the master he carries Caesar's fortune. At Dyrrhachium his men are routed, and he says the enemy would have won if they had had a victor in command, then marches into Macedonia against Scipio.","ideas":["The hesitation and decision at the Rubicon","Clemency at Corfinium encourages men back to Rome","Arms and laws have not the same season","Pompey's failure to follow up at Dyrrhachium"],"terms":[{"t":"Rubicon","d":"The river dividing Cisalpine Gaul from Italy, whose crossing under arms began the civil war."}]},{"i":453,"j":463,"loc":"Life of Caesar, chs. 41–48.4","title":"Pharsalus and the pursuit of Pompey","summary":"Pompey wishes to wear Caesar down without battle, but his officers mock him as King of Kings and force him to fight, while dividing Caesar's offices in advance. Caesar's army recovers at Gomphi, omens point to a reversal of fortune, and at Pharsalus Caesar posts hidden cohorts who drive off Pompey's cavalry by thrusting at their faces. Crassinius dies charging first; Pompey, stunned, flees his camp. Caesar laments that his enemies left him no choice, spares many including Brutus, and portents of the victory are reported at Tralles and Patavium. In Alexandria he turns from Pompey's head in horror and weeps over his seal-ring.","ideas":["Pompey is goaded into battle by his own followers","The cohorts aimed at the faces of the young horsemen","Pompey loses himself and flees: 'What, even to my quarters?'","Caesar's pleasure in sparing fellow citizens"],"terms":[{"t":"lustration","d":"A ritual purification and review of the army, at which the seer foretold a reversal of fortune."}]},{"i":464,"j":476,"loc":"Life of Caesar, chs. 48.5–56","title":"Egypt, Pontus, Africa, and Munda","summary":"Plutarch reports that some blame the Alexandrian war on Caesar's passion for Cleopatra, others on the eunuch Potheinus. Cleopatra is smuggled to Caesar in a bed-sack; Caesar kills Potheinus, survives a hard siege in which the great library burns, swims to safety holding his papers, and leaves Cleopatra on the throne. He crushes Pharnaces at Zela ('Came, saw, conquered'), then fights Scipio and Juba in Africa, winning at Thapsus, though some say a seizure kept him out of the battle. Cato's suicide at Utica leads to the 'Anti-Cato' against Cicero's 'Cato'. After triumphs and a census showing the losses of civil war, he defeats Pompey's sons at Munda, fighting for his very life.","ideas":["The burning of the library in the Alexandrian fighting","Brevity of 'Came, saw, conquered'","Caesar begrudges Cato his death","The census halves: the cost of the civil wars"],"terms":[{"t":"Anti-Cato","d":"Caesar's pamphlet against the dead Cato, which Plutarch attributes to political rivalry with Cicero."}]},{"i":477,"j":483,"loc":"Life of Caesar, chs. 57–62","title":"Dictator for life and the desire for kingship","summary":"The triumph over Pompey's sons offends the Romans, yet they accept Caesar as dictator for life, which Plutarch calls an avowed tyranny. He is mild after the wars, pardoning and promoting enemies such as Brutus and Cassius, restoring Pompey's statues and refusing a bodyguard. His ambition turns to vast projects, a Parthian and Scythian campaign, a canal at Corinth and harbours, and he reforms the calendar. Hatred grows over his wish to be king: he fails to rise for the senate, and at the Lupercalia Antony offers him a diadem, which the people applaud him for refusing. The section ends with the people turning to Marcus Brutus and Cassius urging him on, while Caesar distrusts the pale, thin men.","ideas":["Excessive honours make Caesar odious, some voted by his enemies","Emulation of himself drives ever greater plans","The Julian reform of the calendar","The diadem offered and refused at the Lupercalia"],"terms":[{"t":"Lupercalia","d":"A Roman festival in which naked runners strike bystanders with thongs, the setting of Antony's offer of the diadem."},{"t":"Mercedonius","d":"The intercalary month the priests inserted irregularly before Caesar's calendar reform."}]},{"i":484,"j":495,"loc":"Life of Caesar, chs. 63–69","title":"The Ides of March and their aftermath","summary":"Omens precede the murder: a sacrificial victim without a heart, the seer's warning about the Ides, Calpurnia's dream, and Caesar's remark that the best death is unexpected. Decimus Brutus persuades him to attend the senate, and Artemidorus's warning roll goes unread. In the hall of Pompey the conspirators surround him, Casca strikes first, and Caesar, seeing Brutus, covers his head and falls at Pompey's statue with twenty-three wounds. Brutus's party marches to the Capitol, the senate votes an amnesty, but Caesar's will and the sight of his body inflame the people, who burn him in the forum and tear apart the wrong Cinna. Plutarch closes with Caesar's guardian genius avenging him: Cassius dies by the same dagger, a comet appears, and a phantom visits Brutus before Philippi.","ideas":["Destiny unavoidable rather than unexpected","The murder at the foot of Pompey's statue as if divinely guided","The people pity Caesar but respect Brutus, until the will is read","The evil genius that appears to Brutus"],"terms":[{"t":"Ides of March","d":"The fifteenth of March, the day a seer warned Caesar against."},{"t":"daimōn (guardian genius)","d":"The spirit attending Caesar through life that, Plutarch says, pursued his killers after his death."}]},{"i":496,"j":512,"loc":"Life of Phocion, chs. 1–7","title":"Phocion's virtue, character and early career","summary":"Plutarch opens with Demades' claim to steer a shipwrecked state and argues that Fortune can dim the fame of good men in evil times. He reflects that a city in calamity cannot bear frank speech, and that good government blends austerity with gentleness, as God rules the universe by persuasion. He then pairs Phocion with Cato the Younger as men of one stamp of virtue, whose differences need fine reasoning to find. The narrative turns to Phocion's decent birth, schooling under Plato and Xenocrates, his stern face, terse and forceful speech, and his service under Chabrias at Naxos. It ends with his aim to unite the roles of general and statesman, as Pericles, Aristides and Solon had done.","ideas":["Fortune can obscure virtue by bringing calumny on good men","A suffering people resents frank counsel, yet needs it most","Phocion's speech said the most in the fewest words","Phocion revived the union of general and orator"],"terms":[{"t":"menoeikes","d":"Homeric word for 'pleasant', which Plutarch reads as what yields to the pleasure-loving part of the soul."},{"t":"Phocion the Good","d":"Phocion's surname, which Plutarch finds surprising for so harsh and stern a man."}]},{"i":513,"j":522,"loc":"Life of Phocion, chs. 8–14","title":"Forty-five generalships, sayings and Euboea","summary":"Phocion favours peace in policy yet is elected general forty-five times without ever canvassing, because the Athenians turn to their sternest citizen when they need a commander. A string of his sharp retorts to the assembly, to Demosthenes, Polyeuctus, Archibiades and Aristogeiton shows his harshness, which Plutarch says was never personal enmity. Allies welcome fleets he leads with garlands. In Euboea he holds a ridge near Tamynae, waits on his sacrifices while Plutarch of Eretria rushes out and is routed, then wins the battle, expels Plutarch and frees his Greek prisoners. Later he saves Byzantium, where Chares had failed, and drives Philip out of the Hellespont.","ideas":["Athens chose its flatterers for amusement but Phocion for command","Phocion's harshness served the state, not personal hatred","Victory at Tamynae in Euboea","Relief of Byzantium and Philip's expulsion from the Hellespont"],"terms":[{"t":"stratēgos (general)","d":"The elected Athenian military office that Phocion held forty-five times."}]},{"i":523,"j":533,"loc":"Life of Phocion, chs. 15–22","title":"Phocion between Philip, Alexander and Harpalus","summary":"Phocion leads a sudden expedition to Megara and links it to the sea by long walls. He urges acceptance of Philip's peace, opposes Demosthenes' war policy, takes charge of the city after Chaeroneia, and refuses to rejoice at Philip's death. After the fall of Thebes he counsels yielding to Alexander, wins him over, and is honoured by him, but refuses his hundred talents and the revenues of Asian cities, asking only for the release of prisoners. His wife's plainness, his care for his dissolute son Phocus, and his refusal of Harpalus' seven hundred talents illustrate his integrity. The section ends with him calming the assembly at the news of Alexander's death.","ideas":["Phocion advised Alexander to turn his war against the barbarians","He refused Alexander's gifts, saying a poor man had less and was content","His wife called Phocion her only ornament","He would not defend his son-in-law Charicles over Harpalus' money"],"terms":[{"t":"chairein","d":"Greek word of greeting that Alexander, after defeating Dareius, kept only in letters to Phocion and Antipater."},{"t":"agōgē","d":"Spartan course of youth discipline to which Phocion sent his son."}]},{"i":534,"j":545,"loc":"Life of Phocion, chs. 23–30","title":"The Lamian war and Antipater's peace","summary":"Phocion opposes Leosthenes' Lamian war, warning that Athens could win the short race but not the long one. After Leosthenes' death he checks an expedition against Boeotia and defeats Micion's Macedonians near Rhamnus. After the Greek defeat at Crannon he is sent to Antipater, who keeps him from invading Attica but imposes harsh terms: surrender of Demosthenes and Hypereides, a property-based constitution, and a garrison in Munychia, which Xenocrates calls fit for slaves. The garrison arrives during the Mysteries amid ill omens, and over twelve thousand poor citizens lose their franchise. Phocion governs mildly, wins exemptions from exile, and refuses gifts from Menyllus and Antipater, in contrast with the greedy Demades.","ideas":["Phocion feared the long course of the war","Antipater's terms ended the full democracy and garrisoned Munychia","Omens at the Eleusinian Mysteries foretold the loss of the harbour","Phocion's poverty set against Demades' display of wealth"],"terms":[{"t":"Munychia","d":"Hill fort of the Peiraeus where the Macedonian garrison was placed."}]},{"i":546,"j":558,"loc":"Life of Phocion, chs. 30–38","title":"Phocion's fall, trial and death","summary":"Phocion declines to seek removal of the garrison, while Demades goes to Macedonia and is killed by Cassander. After Antipater's death Nicanor seizes Munychia; Phocion trusts him too long, which Plutarch criticises as a breach of duty to his city. Polysperchon's restored democracy deposes Phocion, who goes to Polysperchon and is sent back to Athens to be tried by a mob assembly. Condemned by show of hands, he meets death calmly, telling his son to bear no grudge and paying for the last of the hemlock. His body is cast out, his wife buries his bones by the hearth, and Athens later honours him with a statue, a reflection of Socrates' fate.","ideas":["Phocion's misplaced trust in Nicanor","A rigged assembly of slaves, foreigners and disfranchised condemns him","Calm death by hemlock on a festival day","Later remorse: statue, public burial, death of Hagnonides"],"terms":[{"t":"cheirotonia (show of hands)","d":"The open vote by which the assembly condemned Phocion and his friends."}]},{"i":559,"j":573,"loc":"Life of Cato the Younger, chs. 1–7","title":"Cato's childhood, training and first marriage","summary":"Orphaned, Cato grows up in the house of his uncle Livius Drusus, showing from boyhood an inflexible and steadfast nature, slow to learn but tenacious. He refuses to be frightened by Pompaedius Silo, is chosen leader by the boys in the Troy game, and as a boy asks for a sword to kill Sulla. Devoted to his brother Caepio, he studies Stoic ethics with Antipater of Tyre and makes his first speech defending the Basilica Porcia. He hardens his body, goes about without shoes or tunic, lends his inheritance without interest, and deliberately opposes the fashions of the age. Jilted by Lepida, he writes iambics against Scipio and marries Atilia.","ideas":["Slow learning but firm memory and a demand for reasons","The boy Cato's wish to free Rome from Sulla","Stoic training and a deliberately austere life","Contrary habits meant to feel shame only for what is shameful"],"terms":[{"t":"Stoicism","d":"The philosophy Cato studied, valuing rigid justice above clemency or favour."},{"t":"Troja","d":"Sacred equestrian game for noble boys, in which the boys chose Cato as leader."}]},{"i":574,"j":584,"loc":"Life of Cato the Younger, chs. 8–15","title":"Military tribune and travels in Asia","summary":"Cato serves as a volunteer against Spartacus and refuses the prizes offered him, then as military tribune in Macedonia trains his legion by reason and example until his men love him. He travels to Pergamum to bring the Stoic Athenodorus back to camp, and grieves extravagantly for his brother Caepio at Aenus, refusing gifts of money. Touring Asia with a modest retinue, he is mistaken at Antioch for Pompey's freedman's escort, but Pompey himself honours him at Ephesus. He rejects Deiotarus' gifts and carries Caepio's ashes home through a dangerous voyage.","ideas":["Leadership that made soldiers like himself","Capture of Athenodorus as a nobler prize than kingdoms","Grief for Caepio shows tenderness beneath firmness","Refusal of gifts from Deiotarus"],"terms":[{"t":"nomenclator","d":"Slave who prompted a candidate with voters' names; Cato alone obeyed the law forbidding them."}]},{"i":585,"j":592,"loc":"Life of Cato the Younger, chs. 16–20","title":"The reforming quaestor and senator","summary":"Cato studies the laws before standing for the quaestorship, then subdues the treasury clerks, prosecutes the corrupt ones, and refuses to yield even to the censor Catulus. He collects old debts, pays the state's creditors, rejects false decrees, and makes Sulla's paid killers return their bounty, which leads to their condemnation. He erases a remission his friend Marcellus was pressed into granting and keeps watch on the treasury after leaving office. Always first in the senate and last to leave, he becomes a byword for credibility. Hearing that Metellus Nepos is coming as Pompey's agent, he abandons a trip to Lucania to stand for the tribunate against him.","ideas":["The quaestorship given the dignity of the consulship","Sulla's paid assassins forced to disgorge and then tried","'Not to be believed even if Cato says it' as a proverb","Tribunate sought to block Metellus"],"terms":[{"t":"quaestor","d":"Roman magistrate in charge of the public treasury."},{"t":"tribune of the people","d":"Office whose strength Plutarch calls negative: one tribune's veto can stop all."}]},{"i":593,"j":606,"loc":"Life of Cato the Younger, chs. 21–29","title":"Catiline's conspirators and the fight with Metellus","summary":"Elected tribune, Cato prosecutes Murena for bribery, while Cicero mocks Stoic paradoxes in Murena's defence. In the debate on Catiline's accomplices Caesar persuades the senate to imprisonment, but Cato's angry speech, the only one of his preserved thanks to Cicero's shorthand writers, wins the death sentence. Plutarch digresses on the women of Cato's house: Servilia's love letter to Caesar, the misconduct of his other sister and of Atilia, and his handing of Marcia to Hortensius. Cato then resists Metellus' law recalling Pompey with his army, sitting between Metellus and Caesar on the temple steps and facing stones until Metellus flees to Pompey. Cato then prevents the senate from deposing him.","ideas":["Cato's speech carried the execution of the conspirators","Origin of Roman shorthand","The Marcia–Hortensius episode, told out of order","Cato's stand against Metellus' armed gang"],"terms":[{"t":"Stoic paradoxes","d":"Stoic claims contrary to common opinion, mocked by Cicero at Murena's trial."}]},{"i":607,"j":613,"loc":"Life of Cato the Younger, chs. 29–34","title":"Against Pompey, Caesar and Clodius","summary":"Cato secures Lucullus' triumph and blocks Pompey's demand to postpone the consular elections. He rejects Pompey's offer of marriage alliance; Plutarch judges this a mistake, since it drove Pompey to Caesar. Cato's obstruction of Caesar's triumph and candidacy pushes Caesar into union with Pompey, and as consul Caesar forces through land laws amid violence; Cato swears the required oath only after Cicero persuades him. Caesar gets Gaul for five years, and Clodius, made tribune, removes Cato by an honourable-seeming mission to Cyprus.","ideas":["Refused marriage alliance with Pompey and its consequences","Cato's filibuster drives Caesar to Pompey","Cicero persuades Cato that Rome needs him","The Cyprus mission as a device to remove Cato"],"terms":[{"t":"triumph","d":"Victory procession for which a general had to stay outside the city, which forced Caesar to choose between it and the consulship."}]},{"i":614,"j":620,"loc":"Life of Cato the Younger, chs. 35–40","title":"The Cyprus mission and return","summary":"Cato advises Cicero to yield to exile, and at Rhodes receives Ptolemy of Egypt, rebuking him for going to Rome and urging reconciliation with his people. After the Cypriot king poisons himself, Cato settles Byzantium and sells the royal treasures with suspicious exactness, offending friends; Munatius' account of their quarrel and reconciliation through Marcia follows. He brings home nearly seven thousand talents, though both account books are lost. Rome greets him like a triumph; he declines honours but gets freedom for the steward Nicias. He breaks with Cicero by defending the legality of Clodius' tribunate.","ideas":["Cato's lecture to Ptolemy on Roman rapacity","Strict selling of the royal goods","Quarrel with Munatius as revealing character","The treasure with corks and lost accounts"],"terms":[]},{"i":621,"j":628,"loc":"Life of Cato the Younger, chs. 41–44","title":"Resisting the triumvirs; Cato as praetor","summary":"Pompey and Crassus plan joint consulships and huge commands; Cato backs Domitius, who flees when they are ambushed in the Campus Martius. Cato's run for the praetorship is wrecked by Pompey's false report of thunder and bribery that elects Vatinius. He speaks two hours against Trebonius' law and warns Pompey he is taking Caesar on his shoulders. Elected praetor the next year, he forces magistrates-elect to account for their elections and serves as umpire of a pact among candidates. Plutarch observes that justice, because the many love and trust the just, arouses more envy than any other virtue.","ideas":["Pompey's false thunder blocks Cato's election","Warning that Caesar will be Pompey's burden","Cato as trusted umpire of the candidates' deposit","Justice draws more love and envy than courage or wisdom"],"terms":[{"t":"praetor","d":"Roman magistrate below the consul, presiding over courts."}]},{"i":629,"j":634,"loc":"Life of Cato the Younger, chs. 45–50","title":"Pompey's sole consulship and Cato's defeat","summary":"Cato answers Clodius' charges over the Cyprus treasure and accuses Pompey of lending Caesar legions and seeking monarchy through anarchy. He runs Favonius' aedileship with cheap prizes of wild olive and vegetables, to popular delight. Amid electoral violence he supports making Pompey sole consul as the mildest remedy, then offers counsel only in the state's interest and serves as a feared juror. He stands for the consulship to strip Caesar of his army but loses because he will not court the people; he bears it calmly and refuses to change his manners.","ideas":["'Any government is better than none'","Simple games as a rebuke to lavish spectacle","Cato's refusal to canvass costs him the consulship","Defeat met with ball-play in the Campus Martius"],"terms":[{"t":"aedile","d":"Roman magistrate who managed public games, the office held by Favonius."}]},{"i":635,"j":649,"loc":"Life of Cato the Younger, chs. 51–58","title":"Civil war from Rome to Africa","summary":"Cato urges handing Caesar over to the Germans he wronged and exposes his designs; when Caesar takes Ariminum, Pompey admits Cato foresaw it. Cato remarries Marcia, Hortensius' wealthy widow, and Plutarch rebuts Caesar's charge of greed. Mourning for his country, he yields Sicily, persuades Pompey not to sack Roman cities, is denied the fleet, inspires troops before Dyrrhachium, and weeps over the slaughter. After Pharsalus and Pompey's death he leads the remnant across the desert to Libya, refuses supreme command in favour of Scipio, saves Utica from destruction and fortifies it. News of the rout at Thapsus closes the section.","ideas":["Cato as prophet of Caesar's tyranny","Remarriage to Marcia and Caesar's slanders","Desert march and refusal to outrank a proconsul","Utica preserved and fortified"],"terms":[{"t":"Psylli","d":"African people who cured snakebite and charmed serpents, taken on Cato's march."}]},{"i":650,"j":664,"loc":"Life of Cato the Younger, chs. 59–65","title":"Utica after Thapsus","summary":"Cato calms the city by night and addresses his council of three hundred Roman merchants, leaving them free to choose resistance or submission. They first rally to him, but soon waver, fearing Caesar and loath to free their slaves, and some plot against the senators. Cato persuades the fleeing cavalry to stay a day for the senators' safety and rejects their demand to slaughter the Uticans. Having secretly resolved to die, he tells the three hundred to seek Caesar's mercy but not for him, since he is unconquered. On Caesar's approach he organises the senators' escape by sea.","ideas":["Cato refuses to free slaves unlawfully","The merchants' courage cools like porous bodies","Cato pleads with the horsemen to protect the senators","He calls himself the true victor over Caesar"],"terms":[]},{"i":665,"j":682,"loc":"Life of Cato the Younger, chs. 65–73","title":"The death of Cato","summary":"Cato stops the plundering horsemen, oversees the embarkation, and refuses Lucius Caesar's offer to plead for him. At his last supper he defends the Stoic paradox that only the good man is free, then reads Plato's dialogue On the Soul, demands his hidden sword, and rebukes his son and friends. Near dawn, after checking the ships, he stabs himself, and when a physician sews the wound he tears it open and dies. Utica buries him as its saviour; Caesar says he begrudges Cato his death. Plutarch closes with Cato's son, who dies bravely at Philippi, his daughter Porcia, and Statyllius.","ideas":["Last supper debate: only the good man is free","Reading Plato's On the Soul before death","Suicide at Utica and the reopened wound","Caesar's words on being denied the chance to spare him"],"terms":[{"t":"'On the Soul' (Plato)","d":"The Platonic dialogue Cato read through twice on his last night, after his sword was returned."}]},{"i":683,"j":704,"loc":"Life of Agis, chs. 1–11","title":"Agis begins restoring Lycurgus' Sparta","summary":"Plutarch opens with the myth of Ixion to warn that glory won from the multitude makes leaders its slaves, and announces the pairing of the Spartan kings Agis and Cleomenes with the Gracchi. He traces the royal lines of Agis and Leonidas and explains Sparta's decline: wealth entered after the defeat of Athens, and the law of Epitadeus let estates pass freely, leaving only about seven hundred Spartans, a hundred with land. The young Agis adopts the old discipline and, backed by Lysander, Mandrocleidas, his uncle Agesilaüs and the wealthy women of his family, proposes cancelling debts and redividing the land into 4,500 and 15,000 lots. Leonidas leads the rich against him and the bill fails by one vote, but the ephor Lysander indicts Leonidas, who flees as a suppliant, is deposed and replaced by his son-in-law Cleombrotus.","ideas":["Seeking popular glory makes the ruler a servant of the crowd","Greed and the Epitadean law destroyed Lycurgan equality of estates","Agis offers his own fortune to the common stock","Leonidas is deposed through an ancient law and a sign from the sky"],"terms":[{"t":"rhetra","d":"A Spartan enactment; here Agis' bill for remission of debts and redivision of land."},{"t":"ephors","d":"The five annual Spartan magistrates who could indict and suspend kings."}]},{"i":705,"j":719,"loc":"Life of Agis, chs. 12–21","title":"Agesilaüs' avarice and the death of Agis","summary":"Facing prosecution, Lysander and Mandrocleidas persuade the two kings to depose the ephors; Agis protects Leonidas on his flight to Tegea. Agesilaüs, deep in debt but rich in land, has the mortgage bonds burned and then stalls the land division while Agis campaigns beside Aratus near Corinth, winning praise for his disciplined army. In his absence Agesilaüs' extortions turn the people, Leonidas is brought back, and Chilonis wins her husband Cleombrotus' life by her devotion. Agis, lured out of sanctuary by Amphares and others, is tried by the ephors, refuses to repent, and is strangled, followed by his grandmother Archidamia and his mother Agesistrata; Plutarch calls it the first killing of a Spartan king by the ephors.","ideas":["The burning of the debt bonds without the land division","Agis' restrained, admired campaign with Aratus","Chilonis shares the exile of whichever man is wronged","Agis dies unrepentant, judging himself better than his murderers"],"terms":[{"t":"klaria","d":"The Spartan name for mortgage bonds on allotments, burned in the market-place."},{"t":"Dechas","d":"The chamber of the Spartan prison where condemned men were strangled."}]},{"i":720,"j":747,"loc":"Life of Cleomenes, chs. 1–13","title":"Cleomenes seizes power and restores the discipline","summary":"Leonidas forces Agis' widow Agiatis to marry his young son Cleomenes, who learns of Agis' plans from her and is fired further by the Stoic Sphaerus. As king he provokes war with Aratus and the Achaean League, outwits and defeats them at Mt. Lycaeum and Leuctra, and sees Archidamus murdered on his return. Encouraged by an ephor's dream, he marches on Sparta with mercenaries, has four ephors killed at their mess, exiles eighty citizens, and defends the act from Lycurgus' own use of force. Land is pooled and redistributed, the citizen body is refilled, the army rearmed with the long pike, and the agoge restored, while Plutarch describes Cleomenes' plain table and approachable manner.","ideas":["Spartans honour Fear as the support of their polity","The ephors as usurpers of royal power, in Cleomenes' account","Reform achieved by a coup, justified by Lycurgus' precedent","A king who wins men by conversation rather than gifts"],"terms":[{"t":"agōgē","d":"The ancient Spartan system of training, revived by Cleomenes with Sphaerus' help."},{"t":"mothakes","d":"Helots raised alongside free Spartan boys; two of them help kill the ephors."}]},{"i":748,"j":761,"loc":"Life of Cleomenes, chs. 14–22","title":"Aratus calls in Macedon against Cleomenes","summary":"Cleomenes takes Mantineia, routs the Achaeans at Dymae, and demands leadership of the League; illness prevents the settlement at Lerna. Plutarch blames Aratus for inviting Antigonus and the Macedonians into the Peloponnese rather than yield to a Spartan king, while pitying the flaw in so great a man. Cleomenes then seizes Argos during the Nemean games, wins Corinth and much of the Peloponnese, and holds Antigonus at the Oneian hills, until an Argive revolt forces him back and his gains collapse. He learns of his wife Agiatis' death, and his mother Cratesicleia goes willingly to Egypt as a hostage to Ptolemy, urging him to act for Sparta regardless of her.","ideas":["Aratus prefers Macedonian rule to Spartan leadership","The capture of Argos, which earlier kings could never hold","Swift conquests lost almost as quickly","Cratesicleia's composure on leaving for Egypt"],"terms":[]},{"i":762,"j":772,"loc":"Life of Cleomenes, chs. 23–30","title":"Megalopolis, Sellasia, and the fall of Sparta","summary":"Freeing helots for cash, Cleomenes surprises and enters Megalopolis; he offers to return it if the citizens leave the Achaeans, and when Philopoemen refuses, he sacks it. He ravages the Argolid in defiance of Antigonus, who refuses to fight. Plutarch observes that money is the sinews of war: short of funds, Cleomenes must stake everything on the battle of Sellasia just before Antigonus is called home. Betrayed according to Phylarchus and with his brother Eucleidas' wing surrounded, he loses all but two hundred of six thousand Spartans, advises the city to receive Antigonus, and sails from Gythium; Antigonus treats Sparta humanely and soon dies in Macedonia.","ideas":["Clemency offered at Megalopolis, then anger in its destruction","Antigonus' prudent refusal to be shamed into battle","Fortune decides Sellasia by a margin of two days","Cleomenes' silent grief before his flight"],"terms":[]},{"i":773,"j":788,"loc":"Life of Cleomenes, chs. 31–39","title":"Exile in Egypt and the deaths of Cleomenes' house","summary":"Therycion urges suicide, but Cleomenes replies that a self-inflicted death should be an act, not a flight, and sails to Ptolemy, who honours and pensions him. Under the dissolute Ptolemy Philopator, the minister Sosibius and a forged charge from Nicagoras lead to his confinement. Overhearing himself called a dangerous wild beast, Cleomenes and thirteen friends break out, kill two officials, call vainly on the Alexandrians to rise, and die by their own hands, Panteus last. Ptolemy has his body flayed and his mother, children and the women killed; the wife of Panteus tends the dying with great composure, and a serpent guarding Cleomenes' corpse leads Alexandrians to hail him as a hero until it is explained naturally.","ideas":["Suicide as flight versus suicide as action","A Spartan king caged at a corrupt court","The failed rising in Alexandria","Women's deaths matching the men's in virtue"],"terms":[]},{"i":789,"j":806,"loc":"Life of Tiberius Gracchus, chs. 1–11","title":"Tiberius Gracchus and the agrarian law","summary":"Plutarch introduces the sons of Tiberius Gracchus and Cornelia, contrasts the gentle Tiberius with the vehement Caius, and notes their nine-year age gap. Tiberius serves at Carthage, and at Numantia saves twenty thousand Romans by a truce the Senate disowns. Seizure of public land by the rich and the dearth of free farmers drive him, as tribune, to propose a mild land law; he speaks for the landless soldiers who have no clod of their own. When his colleague Marcus Octavius vetoes it, Tiberius brings a harsher bill, halts public business, and the rich steal the voting urns; the matter is referred to the Senate.","ideas":["Twin natures differing in temper and oratory","The Numantine treaty and Tiberius' popularity","Italy emptied of free men and worked by slaves","The tribunician veto blocks the law"],"terms":[{"t":"ager publicus (public land)","d":"Conquered land held by the state, which the rich had engrossed beyond the 500-acre limit."},{"t":"tribune","d":"Sacrosanct officer of the plebs, any one of whom could veto measures."}]},{"i":807,"j":824,"loc":"Life of Tiberius Gracchus, chs. 11–21","title":"Octavius deposed and Tiberius killed","summary":"After the Senate fails to act, Tiberius has the people vote Octavius out of office, pleading with him tearfully at the seventeenth tribe. The land law passes with a commission of three, but Nasica and the Senate humiliate him, and his plan for Attalus' bequest and Annius' question about deposed tribunes deepen hostility. Tiberius defends the deposition at length and seeks a second tribunate; bad omens precede the assembly on the Capitol. Told the rich intend to kill him, he signals danger by touching his head, which is reported as a demand for a crown; Nasica leads senators who club him and three hundred others to death. The bodies are thrown in the river, his friends punished, and Nasica driven abroad by popular hatred.","ideas":["A tribune who harms the people is no tribune, Tiberius argues","The gesture misread as a bid for kingship","The first civil bloodshed at Rome since the kings","Blossius' defence of loyal obedience to Tiberius"],"terms":[{"t":"pontifex maximus","d":"Rome's chief priest; Nasica held the office yet fled Italy after the killing."}]},{"i":825,"j":837,"loc":"Life of Caius Gracchus, chs. 1–8","title":"Caius Gracchus enters public life","summary":"Caius at first withdraws, but his oratory alarms the nobles, and Cicero reports a dream in which Tiberius tells him one life and death await them both. As quaestor in Sardinia he excels and secures clothing for the troops, returns early, and defends himself before the censors. Elected tribune, he recalls his brother's murder and passes laws on deposed magistrates and banishment without trial, sparing Octavius at Cornelia's request. His programme includes land, soldiers' clothing, cheap grain, Italian suffrage, and adding equestrians to the juries; he turns to face the people when speaking and builds fine measured roads. He secures Fannius' consulship and a second tribunate, but the Senate sets Livius Drusus to outbid him.","ideas":["Caius' reluctance overcome by necessity and the dream","Juries shared between senators and equestrians","Turning from the Senate to the people as a constitutional gesture","Tireless public works, especially roads"],"terms":[{"t":"comitium","d":"The area of the Forum before the senate-house toward which earlier orators faced."},{"t":"equestrian order","d":"Wealthy non-senatorial Romans, three hundred of whom Caius added to the judges."}]},{"i":838,"j":851,"loc":"Life of Caius Gracchus, chs. 9–19","title":"Drusus outbids Caius; Caius dies","summary":"Livius Drusus, with Senate backing, proposes twelve colonies and other favours, eroding Caius' support while he founds Junonia at Carthage amid bad omens. Returning, Caius moves near the Forum, loses favour by pulling down rented seats for a gladiator show, and fails of a third tribunate; Opimius becomes consul and attacks his laws. The killing of the attendant Antyllius gives Opimius his pretext, the Senate charges him to save the state, and Fulvius' drunken partisans seize the Aventine. Ignoring Licinia's plea, Caius goes out unarmed; after the rout he flees across the Tiber and dies in the grove of the Furies, and his head is sold for its weight in gold. Opimius builds a temple of Concord, later dies in disgrace, and the people honour the brothers while Cornelia bears her loss nobly.","ideas":["The Senate opposes the man rather than his measures","Fulvius' rashness drags Caius down","The first consul to wield a dictator's power, killing without trial","Cornelia's calm as proof virtue can endure fortune"],"terms":[{"t":"Junonia","d":"The colony Caius founded on the site of Carthage."}]},{"i":852,"j":858,"loc":"Comparison of Agis and Cleomenes and the Gracchi","title":"Comparing the Spartan kings and the Gracchi","summary":"Plutarch judges the Spartans' natures sturdier, since they rose to simplicity without good training, and their reforms bolder, aiming to restore Lycurgus' whole constitution rather than apply partial remedies. Cleomenes made Sparta mistress of the Peloponnese, while Rome gained nothing in greatness from the Gracchi; yet the brothers' military deeds rival his. Agis lacked spirit and was baffled by Agesilaüs, Cleomenes was rash and violent in killing the ephors, while neither Gracchus began civil slaughter. He concludes that Tiberius excelled in virtue, Agis erred least, and Caius fell short of Cleomenes in achievement and courage.","ideas":["Whole-constitution reform versus cutting a Hydra's heads","Manner of death as a test of excellence","The knife as the last resort of physician and statesman","Each of the four ranked on a different virtue"],"terms":[]}]});